<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">What this lesson is about</h2>
<p>A generative model produces new output by choosing among possible results. Those choices are not arbitrary: the model assigns a probability to each possible result and then samples from those probabilities.</p>
<p>We will begin with very small examples&mdash;random numbers and a yes/no outcome&mdash;so you can see every probability and check the results yourself. The same ideas will later be used to choose the next character in the N-gram lesson and, later in the course, the next token produced by a language model.</p>
<p><strong>What you will do:</strong> read the short explanation below, run two Python files, change a few values, and compare what you expected with what the programs produced.</p>
<div style="border-left: 4px solid #941728; background-color: #f7f3f4; padding: 0.85rem 1rem; margin: 1rem 0;">
<p style="margin: 0;"><strong>A note about the mathematics:</strong> You may have used this notation recently, or you may have spent years in industry without needing it. Either situation is normal. The first time an unfamiliar symbol appears, we will name it and tell you how to pronounce it. We will also translate each formula into ordinary language and</p>
<p style="margin: 0;">connect the notation to code. Reading the notation is part of what you are learning here; it is not something you are expected to have memorized before you begin.</p>
</div>
