<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">The ideas you need</h2>
<p>A <strong>random experiment</strong> is something you run when you do not know the result in advance. Rolling a die is a random experiment. Each possible result, such as rolling a 4, is an <strong>outcome</strong>. The complete list of possible outcomes is the <strong>sample space</strong>. One result produced when you run the experiment is a <strong>sample</strong>.</p>
<p>A <strong>random variable</strong> gives a name and a numerical value to the result of a random experiment. We normally use an uppercase letter for the random variable and a lowercase letter for one particular value. For a die roll, we might define <em>X</em> as &ldquo;the number showing on the die.&rdquo; Before the roll, <em>X</em> could be 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, or 6. After the roll, we observe one value, such as <em>x</em> = 4.</p>
<p>An <strong>event</strong> is a statement about the result that can be true or false. For example, <em>X</em> = 4 is the event &ldquo;the die shows 4,&rdquo; while <em>X</em> &gt; 3 is the event &ldquo;the die shows 4, 5, or 6.&rdquo; The notation <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = <em>x</em>) means &ldquo;the probability that random variable <em>X</em> takes the particular value <em>x</em>.&rdquo;</p>
<p>Why is that <em>P</em> uppercase when some formulas below use a lowercase <em>p</em>? The uppercase <em>P</em> asks for the probability of an <strong>event</strong>. It can be used for an event involving one value, such as <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = 4), or several values, such as <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> &gt; 3).</p>
<p>For a <strong>discrete</strong> random variable, lowercase <em>p</em> usually names its <strong>probability mass function</strong>. It assigns a probability to each separate possible value. Strictly speaking, <em>x</em> is a value while <em>X</em> = <em>x</em> is an event, so they are not the same thing. Nevertheless, we will often use the convenient shorthand <em>p</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = <em>x</em>). For a fair die, for example, <em>p</em>(4) = <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = 4) = 1/6. This is a small piece of mathematical sloppiness that is acceptable when the intended meaning is clear.</p>
<p>For a <strong>continuous</strong> random variable, lowercase <em>f</em> usually names a <strong>probability density function</strong>. A density value <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) is not itself the probability of obtaining exactly <em>x</em>. Probabilities come from intervals, such as <em>P</em>(<em>c</em> &le; <em>X</em> &le; <em>d</em>). You may encounter books that use lowercase <em>p</em> for both mass functions and density functions; in this course, we will normally use <em>p</em> for discrete probabilities and <em>f</em> for continuous densities.</p>
<p>A <strong>probability distribution</strong> is simply a rule that says how likely each possible outcome is. For a list of separate outcomes, such as the six sides of a die, two rules must be true:</p>
<p>The large &sum; symbol is the summation symbol, based on the capital Greek letter <strong>sigma</strong> (pronounced &ldquo;SIG-ma&rdquo;). It means &ldquo;add the indicated terms.&rdquo; In Equation (1.1), it tells you to add <em>p</em>(<em>x</em>) for every possible value of <em>x</em>. The equation says that no probability may be negative and that all the probabilities must add to 1.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.1. Every probability is at least zero, and all probabilities sum to one."> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>&ge;</mo> <mn>0</mn> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <munder> <mo>&sum;</mo> <mrow> <mi>x</mi> <mo>&isin;</mo> <mi>𝒳</mi> </mrow> </munder> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.1)</span></div>
<p>A <strong>uniform distribution</strong> gives every outcome the same chance. For example, a fair six-sided die assigns a probability of one-sixth to every side.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.2. For K equally likely outcomes, p of x equals one divided by K."> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mi>K</mi> </mfrac> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.2)</span></div>
<p>A continuous uniform distribution chooses a number from an interval. Because equal-length parts of the interval are equally likely, the probability of landing in a smaller interval is its length divided by the length of the full interval.</p>
<h3>Two possible outcomes: the Bernoulli distribution</h3>
<p>A <strong>Bernoulli distribution</strong> describes one yes/no, success/failure, or 0/1 result. Let <em>X</em> be the random variable and define &mu;&mdash;the Greek letter <strong>mu</strong>, pronounced &ldquo;mew&rdquo;&mdash;by &mu; = <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = 1). In other words, &mu; is the probability of the event <em>X</em> = 1. Then 0 &le; &mu; &le; 1. The corresponding probability mass function has <em>p</em>(1; &mu;) = &mu; and <em>p</em>(0; &mu;) = 1 &minus; &mu;.</p>
<p>The Bernoulli distribution is often written in the compact form below:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.4. p of x given mu equals mu to the x times one minus mu to the one minus x, for x equal to zero or one."> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <msup> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>x</mi> </msup> <msup> <mrow> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>&minus;</mo> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> <mo>&minus;</mo> <mi>x</mi> </mrow> </msup> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <mi>x</mi> <mo>&isin;</mo> <mo stretchy="false">{</mo> <mn>0</mn> <mo>,</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo stretchy="false">}</mo> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.3)</span></div>
<p>This notation is a little sneaky. The exponents are not doing complicated exponential modeling: <em>x</em> can only be 0 or 1. An exponent of 0 turns one factor into 1, while an exponent of 1 leaves the other factor unchanged. The formula is therefore just a compact way to write this casewise statement:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.5. For x equal to zero, p of x given mu equals one minus mu; for x equal to one, it equals mu."> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <mo stretchy="true">{</mo> <mtable columnalign="left"> <mtr> <mtd> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> <mo>&minus;</mo> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <mi>x</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mn>0</mn> </mrow> </mtd> </mtr> <mtr> <mtd> <mrow> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <mi>x</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> </mtd> </mtr> </mtable> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.4)</span></div>
<p>Why write it this way? A single product is convenient in algebra and code, and the same pattern extends to several possible categories. But you should always be able to translate it back into the two cases above.</p>
<h3>More than two possible outcomes: the categorical distribution</h3>
<p>The nonbinary version is called a <strong>categorical distribution</strong> (sometimes a <strong>multinoulli distribution</strong>). Suppose <em>X</em> can take one of <em>K</em> categories. Give category <em>k</em> the probability &mu;<sub><em>k</em></sub> = <em>P</em>(<em>X</em> = <em>k</em>). Read &mu;<sub><em>k</em></sub> as &ldquo;mu sub k.&rdquo; The subscript <em>k</em> identifies which category&rsquo;s probability we mean. Each probability must be nonnegative, and all of them must add to one:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.6. Each mu k is nonnegative; mu one through mu K sum to one; and p of k given mu equals the probability that X equals k, which equals mu k."> <mrow> <msub> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>k</mi> </msub> <mo>&ge;</mo> <mn>0</mn> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <msub> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> <mo>+</mo> <msub> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mo>+</mo> <mo>⋯</mo> <mo>+</mo> <msub> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>K</mi> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>k</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <mi>P</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>X</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mi>k</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <msub> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>k</mi> </msub> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.5)</span></div>
<p>For example, a three-class model might use &mu;<sub>1</sub> = 0.6, &mu;<sub>2</sub> = 0.3, and &mu;<sub>3</sub> = 0.1. The model chooses exactly one of those three categories.</p>
<p>In a <strong>one-hot representation</strong>, the selected category has an indicator value of 1 and every other category has an indicator value of 0.</p>
<p>Equation (1.6) shows the same calculation in two forms. The first line writes out the multiplication one term at a time. The ellipsis, ⋯ (read &ldquo;dot dot dot&rdquo;), means &ldquo;continue the same pattern&rdquo; through the remaining categories.</p>
<p>The second line compresses that multiplication with the large &prod; symbol. This is the product symbol, based on the capital Greek letter <strong>pi</strong> (pronounced &ldquo;pie&rdquo;), and it means &ldquo;multiply the indicated terms.&rdquo; It is not the lowercase &pi; used for the number 3.14159. Because one indicator equals 1 and all the others equal 0, every unselected factor becomes 1. Only the probability of the selected category remains.</p>
<p><strong>How this extends Bernoulli:</strong> for two categories, let &mu;<sub>1</sub> = &mu; and &mu;<sub>2</sub> = 1 &minus; &mu;. Let <em>x</em><sub>1</sub> = <em>x</em> and <em>x</em><sub>2</sub> = 1 &minus; <em>x</em>. The product in Equation (1.6) then becomes &mu; raised to <em>x</em>, multiplied by (1 &minus; &mu;) raised to (1 &minus; <em>x</em>): exactly the Bernoulli formula in Equation (1.4). The categorical equation is the same pattern extended from two categories to <em>K</em> categories.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.7. First, p of vector x given vector mu equals mu one to the x one times mu two to the x two, continuing through mu K to the x K. Equivalently, it equals the product from k equals one to K of mu k raised to x k. Each x k is zero or one, and the x k values sum to one."> <mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.4em"> <mtr> <mtd> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> </mrow> </mtd> <mtd> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <msubsup> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mn>1</mn> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </msubsup> <mo>&times;</mo> <msubsup> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mn>2</mn> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </msubsup> <mo>&times;</mo> <mo>⋯</mo> <mo>&times;</mo> <msubsup> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>K</mi> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mi>K</mi> </msub> </msubsup> </mrow> </mtd> </mtr> <mtr> <mtd> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi mathvariant="bold">x</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mi mathvariant="bold">&mu;</mi> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> </mrow> </mtd> <mtd> <mrow> <mo>=</mo> <munderover> <mo>&prod;</mo> <mrow> <mi>k</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mi>K</mi> </munderover> <msubsup> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mi>k</mi> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mi>k</mi> </msub> </msubsup> </mrow> </mtd> </mtr> <mtr> <mtd></mtd> <mtd> <mrow> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mi>k</mi> </msub> <mo>&isin;</mo> <mo stretchy="false">{</mo> <mn>0</mn> <mo>,</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo stretchy="false">}</mo> <mo>,</mo> <mspace></mspace> <munderover> <mo>&sum;</mo> <mrow> <mi>k</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mi>K</mi> </munderover> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mi>k</mi> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> </mtd> </mtr> </mtable> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.6)</span></div>
<p><strong>One terminology warning:</strong> categorical means choosing exactly one of <em>K</em> categories. For example, if an image must be classified as exactly one of <strong>cat</strong>, <strong>dog</strong>, or <strong>horse</strong>, those three categorical probabilities must sum to one.</p>
<p>A genuinely <strong>multilabel</strong> problem can have several labels present at the same time. A photograph might contain a <strong>person</strong> and a <strong>bicycle</strong> and also be labeled <strong>outdoors</strong>. Each label can be modeled with its own Bernoulli variable: present or absent. Because several labels can be present together, their probabilities do not have to sum to one.</p>
<h3>Estimating an unknown probability</h3>
<p>In a model, &mu; is the true probability that <em>X</em> = 1, but we usually do not know it. We estimate it from observed data. The symbol <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span> is read <strong>&ldquo;mu hat&rdquo;</strong>. The hat is our convention for saying &ldquo;this quantity is an estimate rather than the unknown true value.&rdquo; For Bernoulli data, <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span> is the fraction of observed values that equal 1. The &sum; in Equation (1.7) tells you to add the observed values:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.8. mu hat equals one over n times the sum from i equals one to n of x i."> <mrow> <mover accent="true"> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>^</mo> </mover> <mo>=</mo> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mi>n</mi> </mfrac> <munderover> <mo>&sum;</mo> <mrow> <mi>i</mi> <mo>=</mo> <mn>1</mn> </mrow> <mi>n</mi> </munderover> <msub> <mi>x</mi> <mi>i</mi> </msub> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.7)</span></div>
<p>This estimating procedure is <strong>unbiased</strong>. Imagine repeating the entire sampling process many times, always using the same true &mu; and the same sample size. Some samples will produce an estimate above &mu; and others below it. Unbiased means the procedure has no built-in tendency to run systematically high or systematically low: across many repetitions, the estimates balance around the true &mu;.</p>
<p>Unbiased does <strong>not</strong> mean that one estimate must be close to &mu;, or that overestimates and underestimates must be exactly equally likely. A small sample can still miss badly. Increasing the sample size usually makes the estimates cluster more tightly around &mu;.</p>
<p>After estimating &mu;, we can insert <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span> into the Bernoulli formula to obtain a <strong>fitted model</strong>:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 1.9. The fitted Bernoulli model p of x given mu hat equals mu hat to the x times one minus mu hat to the one minus x."> <mrow> <mi>p</mi> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mi>x</mi> <mo>;</mo> <mover accent="true"> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>^</mo> </mover> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> <mo>=</mo> <msup> <mover accent="true"> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>^</mo> </mover> <mi>x</mi> </msup> <msup> <mrow> <mo stretchy="false">(</mo> <mn>1</mn> <mo>&minus;</mo> <mover accent="true"> <mi>&mu;</mi> <mo>^</mo> </mover> <mo stretchy="false">)</mo> </mrow> <mrow> <mn>1</mn> <mo>&minus;</mo> <mi>x</mi> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(1.8)</span></div>
<p>For this fitted Bernoulli model, <em>p</em>(1; <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span>) = <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span> and <em>p</em>(0; <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span>) = 1 &minus; <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span>. Lesson 3 will show that this same <span aria-label="mu hat">&mu;̂</span> is also the maximum-likelihood estimate.</p>
