<h3>Demo 2: Bernoulli samples</h3>
<p>Download <a href="https://miamioh.instructure.com/courses/243761/files/39953362/download?download_frd=1" data-api-endpoint="https://miamioh.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/243761/files/39953362" data-api-returntype="File"><code>02_bernoulli.py</code> from Canvas</a>, or <a href="https://github.com/jfemiani/cse534-course-demos/blob/master/mathematical_foundations/02_bernoulli.py">read the reviewed source on GitHub</a>. Then run <code>python 02_bernoulli.py</code>. A Bernoulli draw has only two possible outcomes: 0 and 1. The program uses <code>rng.binomial(n=1, p=MU)</code> to generate those draws, prints the first ten trials as a DataFrame, and plots a two-bin histogram. The blue bars show the observed proportions; the red X marks show the probabilities specified by <code>MU</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Try one change:</strong> change <code>MU</code> from <code>0.75</code> to <code>0.25</code>. Predict which bar will become taller, then run the program and check. Here, <code>MU</code> is the probability of outcome 1, so outcome 0 has probability <code>1 - MU</code>.</p>
