<h3>Demo 3: Categorical samples</h3>
<p>Download <a href="https://miamioh.instructure.com/courses/243761/files/39953363/download?download_frd=1" data-api-endpoint="https://miamioh.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/243761/files/39953363" data-api-returntype="File"><code>03_categorical.py</code> from Canvas</a>, or <a href="https://github.com/jfemiani/cse534-course-demos/blob/master/mathematical_foundations/03_categorical.py">read the reviewed source on GitHub</a>. Then run <code>python 03_categorical.py</code>. A categorical draw chooses exactly one item from a list of three or more possibilities. This example chooses <code>cat</code>, <code>dog</code>, or <code>horse</code>. It prints the first ten draws as a DataFrame and uses a bar chart to compare each specified probability with the proportion actually observed.</p>
<p><strong>Try one change:</strong> replace the three values in <code>PROBABILITIES</code> with another nonnegative set that sums to 1, such as <code>[0.20, 0.20, 0.60]</code>. Predict the order of the three bars before running the program. If the probabilities do not sum to 1, the program stops with a deliberately explanatory error rather than silently using an invalid distribution.</p>
